/* ---------- Why ODISSEI matters ---------- */
const WHY={
 intro:[
  `ODISSEI gives social scientists in the Netherlands shared access to data, secure computing, tools and expertise that individual institutions would otherwise have to organise for themselves. More than 45 organisations are members. In 2027, much of this work is carried out through three projects, SSHOC-NL, Macroscope and PDI-SSH, which are coordinated as joint SSH-RI task plans with CLARIAH. This plan describes the work through ODISSEI’s four workstreams: the Data Facility, the Observatory, the Laboratory and the Hub.`,
  `Many research questions about Dutch society depend on data that are sensitive, collected over long periods, or gathered in new ways. Examples are questions on inequality, ageing, education, labour markets, health and digital life. Working with such data requires secure environments, legal agreements, long-term data collection and specialised methods. ODISSEI provides these as shared services, so that researchers at member organisations can use them for their own projects.`],
 closing:`The three projects currently run by ODISSEI have complementary roles: SSHOC-NL builds foundations, Macroscope extends what researchers can do with them, and PDI-SSH helps make the resulting services sustainable. Together they aim for better infrastructure, which supports better research and, through it, a better society.`
};
/* [workstream, title, short summary, full text] */
const CAPS=[
 ["Data Facility","Secure access to sensitive data","CBS microdata, SANE and the OSSC, so institutions do not each build their own.",`CBS microdata, linked administrative records and digital trace data can only be analysed in protected environments. ODISSEI supports access to CBS microdata and offers SANE (Secure Analysis Environment) and the OSSC (ODISSEI Secure Supercomputer) for sensitive data from other providers, so that institutions do not each need to build their own. Data providers keep control over their data, and researchers follow one brokered process. The number of projects on SANE grew from 20 in mid-2025 to 67 in June 2026.`],
 ["Data Facility","Finding and reusing data","The Portal, the knowledge graph and FAIR archiving at DANS.",`The ODISSEI Portal, the knowledge graph and FAIR archiving at DANS help researchers find datasets, connect them, and keep results available for future research. The Portal holds metadata from 19 of ODISSEI’s 27 scientific members and attracts more than 500 unique visitors a month.`],
 ["Observatory","Data collections that follow society over time","LISS, SHARE, ESS, EVS, WVS and GGP, plus PANL for fast recruitment.",`Questions about social mobility, ageing, inequality and family formation need data collected consistently over many years. ODISSEI supports collections that follow the same individuals, households and families over time, and collections that repeat the same questions with new samples to track trends. These include the LISS panel, which is representative of the Dutch population, and the Dutch participation in SHARE (Survey of Health, Ageing and Retirement in Europe), ESS (European Social Survey), EVS (European Values Study), WVS (World Values Survey) and GGP (Gender and Generation Programme), which allows comparison with other countries. PANL adds a flexible platform for fast participant recruitment, and historical records from the Historical Sample of the Netherlands extend the view across generations. The LISS Data Archive has more than 14,000 registered users, and 1,912 publications based on LISS data are known.`],
 ["Laboratory","Tools for new types of data","Data donation, mass online experiments and the Netherlands Media Corpus.",`Researchers increasingly need digital traces, behavioural data and experiments alongside surveys. Through data donation, mass online experiments and the Netherlands Media Corpus, ODISSEI offers shared tools for collecting digital trace data, running large interactive experiments and connecting survey answers with media content. These support research on topics such as social media, misinformation and online behaviour, without each project having to build its own tools.`],
 ["Laboratory","Methods for population-scale analysis","PLANET-NL software for networks that cover the Dutch population.",`PLANET-NL provides open-source software for analysing networks that cover the Dutch population and for assessing the risk that such data reveal individuals. This allows these methods to be used safely within secure environments.`],
 ["Hub","Support, training and coordination","Grants, fellowships, ethics guidance and the Summer School.",`Infrastructure is only useful when researchers can use it with confidence. The Hub offers access grants, fellowships, consultations, ethical and legal guidance, and training such as the ODISSEI-SICSS Summer School. It also coordinates ODISSEI’s work with CLARIAH, SURF, DANS, CBS and European partners.`]
];

